.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt EVP_DIGESTSIGNINIT 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm EVP_DigestSignInit_ex ,
.Nm EVP_DigestSignInit ,
.Nm EVP_DigestSignUpdate ,
.Nm EVP_DigestSignFinal ,
.Nm EVP_DigestSign
.Nd EVP signing functions
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/evp.h
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_DigestSignInit_ex
.Fa "EVP_MD_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "EVP_PKEY_CTX **pctx"
.Fa "const char *mdname"
.Fa "OSSL_LIB_CTX *libctx"
.Fa "const char *props"
.Fa "EVP_PKEY *pkey"
.Fa "const OSSL_PARAM params[]"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_DigestSignInit
.Fa "EVP_MD_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "EVP_PKEY_CTX **pctx"
.Fa "const EVP_MD *type"
.Fa "ENGINE *e"
.Fa "EVP_PKEY *pkey"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_DigestSignUpdate
.Fa "EVP_MD_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "const void *d"
.Fa "size_t cnt"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_DigestSignFinal
.Fa "EVP_MD_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "unsigned char *sig"
.Fa "size_t *siglen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_DigestSign
.Fa "EVP_MD_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "unsigned char *sig"
.Fa "size_t *siglen"
.Fa "const unsigned char *tbs"
.Fa "size_t tbslen"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The EVP signature routines are a high-level interface to digital
signatures.
Input data is digested first before the signing takes place.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_DigestSignInit_ex
sets up signing context
.Fa ctx
to use a digest with the name
.Fa mdname
and private key
.Fa pkey .
The name of the digest to be used is passed to the provider of the
signature algorithm in use.
How that provider interprets the digest name is provider specific.
The provider may implement that digest directly itself or it may
(optionally) choose to fetch it (which could result in a digest from a
different provider being selected).
If the provider supports fetching the digest then it may use the
.Fa props
argument for the properties to be used during the fetch.
Finally, the passed parameters
.Em params ,
if not NULL, are set on the context before returning.
.Pp
The
.Fa pkey
algorithm is used to fetch a
.Sy EVP_SIGNATURE
method implicitly, to be used for the actual signing.
See
.Xr provider 7
for more information about implicit fetches.
.Pp
The OpenSSL default and legacy providers support fetching digests and
can fetch those digests from any available provider.
The OpenSSL FIPS provider also supports fetching digests but will only
fetch digests that are themselves implemented inside the FIPS provider.
.Pp
.Fa ctx
must be created with
.Xr EVP_MD_CTX_new 3
before calling this function.
If
.Fa pctx
is not NULL, the EVP_PKEY_CTX of the signing operation will be written
to
.Em *pctx :
this can be used to set alternative signing options.
Note that any existing value in
.Em *pctx
is overwritten.
The EVP_PKEY_CTX value returned must not be freed directly by the
application if
.Fa ctx
is not assigned an EVP_PKEY_CTX value before being passed to
.Fn EVP_DigestSignInit_ex
(which means the EVP_PKEY_CTX is created inside
.Fn EVP_DigestSignInit_ex
and it will be freed automatically when the EVP_MD_CTX is freed).
If the EVP_PKEY_CTX to be used is created by EVP_DigestSignInit_ex then
it will use the
.Vt OSSL_LIB_CTX
specified in
.Fa libctx
and the property query string specified in
.Fa props .
.Pp
The digest
.Fa mdname
may be NULL if the signing algorithm supports it.
The
.Fa props
argument can always be NULL.
.Pp
No
.Vt EVP_PKEY_CTX
will be created by
.Fn EVP_DigestSignInit_ex
if the passed
.Fa ctx
has already been assigned one via
.Xr EVP_MD_CTX_set_pkey_ctx 3 .
See also
.Xr SM2 7 .
.Pp
Only EVP_PKEY types that support signing can be used with these
functions.
This includes MAC algorithms where the MAC generation is considered as a
form of "signing".
Built-in EVP_PKEY types supported by these functions are CMAC, Poly1305,
DSA, ECDSA, HMAC, RSA, SipHash, Ed25519 and Ed448.
.Pp
Not all digests can be used for all key types.
The following combinations apply.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It DSA
Supports SHA1, SHA224, SHA256, SHA384 and SHA512
.It ECDSA
Supports SHA1, SHA224, SHA256, SHA384, SHA512 and SM3
.It RSA with no padding
Supports no digests (the digest
.Fa type
must be NULL)
.It RSA with X931 padding
Supports SHA1, SHA256, SHA384 and SHA512
.It All other RSA padding types
Support SHA1, SHA224, SHA256, SHA384, SHA512, MD5, MD5_SHA1, MD2, MD4,
MDC2, SHA3-224, SHA3-256, SHA3-384, SHA3-512
.It Ed25519 and Ed448
Support no digests (the digest
.Fa type
must be NULL)
.It HMAC
Supports any digest
.It CMAC, Poly1305 and SipHash
Will ignore any digest provided.
.El
.Pp
If RSA-PSS is used and restrictions apply then the digest must match.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_DigestSignInit
works in the same way as
.Fn EVP_DigestSignInit_ex
except that the
.Fa mdname
parameter will be inferred from the supplied digest
.Fa type ,
and
.Fa props
will be NULL. Where supplied the ENGINE
.Fa e
will be used for the signing and digest algorithm implementations.
.Fa e
may be NULL.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_DigestSignUpdate
hashes
.Fa cnt
bytes of data at
.Fa d
into the signature context
.Fa ctx .
This function can be called several times on the same
.Fa ctx
to include additional data.
.Fa ctx
.Sy MUST NOT
be NULL.
.Pp
Unless
.Fa sig
is NULL
.Fn EVP_DigestSignFinal
signs the data in
.Fa ctx
and places the signature in
.Fa sig .
Otherwise the maximum necessary size of the output buffer is written to
the
.Fa siglen
parameter.
If
.Fa sig
is not NULL then before the call the
.Fa siglen
parameter should contain the length of the
.Fa sig
buffer.
If the call is successful the signature is written to
.Fa sig
and the amount of data written to
.Fa siglen .
.Pp
.Fn EVP_DigestSign
is similar to a single call to
.Fn EVP_DigestSignUpdate
and
.Fn EVP_DigestSignFinal .
Unless
.Fa sig
is NULL,
.Fn EVP_DigestSign
signs the data
.Fa tbs
of length
.Fa tbslen
bytes and places the signature in a buffer
.Fa sig
of size
.Fa siglen .
If
.Fa sig
is NULL, the maximum necessary size of the signature buffer is written
to the
.Fa siglen
parameter.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn EVP_DigestSignInit ,
.Fn EVP_DigestSignUpdate ,
.Fn EVP_DigestSignFinal
and
.Fn EVP_DigestSign
return 1 for success and 0 for failure.
.Pp
The error codes can be obtained from
.Xr ERR_get_error 3 .
.Sh NOTES
The
.Sy EVP
interface to digital signatures should almost always be used in
preference to the low-level interfaces.
This is because the code then becomes transparent to the algorithm used
and much more flexible.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_DigestSign
is a one shot operation which signs a single block of data in one
function.
For algorithms that support streaming it is equivalent to calling
.Fn EVP_DigestSignUpdate
and
.Fn EVP_DigestSignFinal .
For algorithms which do not support streaming (e.g.
PureEdDSA) it is the only way to sign data.
.Pp
In previous versions of OpenSSL there was a link between message digest
types and public key algorithms.
This meant that "clone" digests such as
.Xr EVP_dss1 3
needed to be used to sign using SHA1 and DSA. This is no longer
necessary and the use of clone digest is now discouraged.
.Pp
For some key types and parameters the random number generator must be
seeded.
If the automatic seeding or reseeding of the OpenSSL CSPRNG fails due to
external circumstances (see
.Xr RAND 7 Ns ),
the operation will fail.
.Pp
The call to
.Fn EVP_DigestSignFinal
internally finalizes a copy of the digest context.
This means that calls to
.Fn EVP_DigestSignUpdate
and
.Fn EVP_DigestSignFinal
can be called later to digest and sign additional data.
Applications may disable this behavior by setting the
EVP_MD_CTX_FLAG_FINALISE context flag via
.Xr EVP_MD_CTX_set_flags 3 .
.Pp
Note that not all providers support continuation, in case the selected
provider does not allow to duplicate contexts
.Fn EVP_DigestSignFinal
will finalize the digest context and attempting to process additional
data via
.Fn EVP_DigestSignUpdate
will result in an error.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_DigestSignInit
and
.Fn EVP_DigestSignInit_ex
functions can be called multiple times on a context and the parameters
set by previous calls should be preserved if the
.Fa pkey
parameter is NULL. The call then just resets the state of the
.Fa ctx .
.Pp
.Fn EVP_DigestSign
can not be called again, once a signature is generated (by passing
.Fa sig
as non NULL), unless the
.Vt EVP_MD_CTX
is reinitialised by calling
.Fn EVP_DigestSignInit_ex .
.Pp
Ignoring failure returns of
.Fn EVP_DigestSignInit
and
.Fn EVP_DigestSignInit_ex
functions can lead to subsequent undefined behavior when calling
.Fn EVP_DigestSignUpdate ,
.Fn EVP_DigestSignFinal ,
or
.Fn EVP_DigestSign .
.Pp
The use of
.Xr EVP_PKEY_get_size 3
with these functions is discouraged because some signature operations
may have a signature length which depends on the parameters set.
As a result
.Xr EVP_PKEY_get_size 3
would have to return a value which indicates the maximum possible
signature for any set of parameters.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr EVP_DigestVerifyInit 3 ,
.Xr EVP_DigestInit 3 ,
.Xr evp 7 ,
.Xr HMAC 3 ,
.Xr MD2 3 ,
.Xr MD5 3 ,
.Xr MDC2 3 ,
.Xr RIPEMD160 3 ,
.Xr SHA1 3 ,
.Xr openssl-dgst 1 ,
.Xr RAND 7
.Sh HISTORY
.Fn EVP_DigestSignInit ,
.Fn EVP_DigestSignUpdate
and
.Fn EVP_DigestSignFinal
were added in OpenSSL 1.0.0.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_DigestSignInit_ex
was added in OpenSSL 3.0.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_DigestSignUpdate
was converted from a macro to a function in OpenSSL 3.0.
